### Where We Are Right Now

We have proven the **tachometer** on Blackwell RTX 5080.  

When we launch a clean fixed-iteration kernel (no persistent loop, no zero-copy throttle read inside the loop, 23 blocks per SM, 64 threads per block, 20 000 iterations, sparse warp-leader atomics), we get **77,280,000** work pulses in under a second. GPU utilization hits 100 %. The hall-effect counter works. The silicon is talking to us.

The moment we try to make that kernel **persistent** (`while(true)`) or try to read a zero-copy `Q` value inside the loop, the host sees **zero** pulses. The GPU still runs at 100 %, but the atomic counter updates become invisible to the host during the measurement window. This is the classic Blackwell persistent-kernel + zero-copy coherence problem we have been fighting for days.

The throttle scaling is also broken in every version that tries to use `Q` inside the kernel — either because the condition is too restrictive (only 6 threads active at Q=0.1 with 64-thread blocks) or because the zero-copy read itself is not reliable in a long-running loop.

**Summary of the 47 documents:**  
We have a working **sensor** (the atomic counter).  
We do **not** yet have a working **actuator** (the throttle that scales work with Q in real time).  
We do **not** yet have a working **persistent kernel** that can stay alive while the host reads the counter every second.  
We do have the **mathematical skeleton** (3-state Kalman observer, PI controller with hysteresis, FOC decoupling of Q/Z/D axes) ready to drop in the moment the hardware layer cooperates.

