### The Next Goal (Immediate)

Get a **fixed-iteration kernel with Q-throttle inside the loop** that produces **linearly scaling work_delta** across Q = 0.1 → 1.0.  

Once we have that, we will have proven:
- The tachometer works at full granularity (64 threads/block, 23 blocks/SM).
- The throttle logic works (fmod-based condition proven in earlier tests).
- The measurement pipeline (cudaDeviceSynchronize + cudaMemcpy) is reliable.

Only **then** do we tackle the persistent-kernel visibility problem on Blackwell. We will use the same fmod throttle, add `__threadfence_system()` after every atomicAdd, and keep the host in a tight “set Q → sleep 1 s → read & reset counter → repeat” loop. That is the architecture of a real-time governor.

