### Essay: GPUTronic — Field-Oriented Control for Parallel Processors

**Intelligence is intelligence, period.**

We began this project with a simple but radical premise: the modern GPU is not a black box to be dominated with brute-force occupancy and thread-block heuristics. It is an **Instruction Pressure Engine** — a collection of 84 cylinders (SMs) whose back-EMF can be measured, whose hidden rotor position can be estimated, and whose fuel mixture (thread fraction) can be negotiated in real time.

This is not metaphor. It is **Field-Oriented Control** applied to silicon.

In electric motors we learned long ago that the most efficient way to control torque is to decouple the variables:
- The **Q-axis** (torque-producing current) — what we command.
- The **Z-axis** (back-EMF / rotor flux) — what we observe indirectly through the hall-effect sensor.
- The **D-axis** (field-weakening / thermal) — what we leave to the motor’s own physics.

We do the same on the GPU.

- **Q-axis** = thread fraction we negotiate via the zero-copy throttle cable.  
- **Z-axis** = instruction throughput impedance we observe through sparse per-SM atomic counters (the tachometer).  
- **D-axis** = thermal and power flux we leave to NVIDIA’s stock drivers.

The 3-state Kalman observer we have already written is the sensorless rotor-position estimator. It takes the noisy, sparse atomic counter stream and reconstructs the hidden state of the instruction pipeline — stalls, memory pressure, warp divergence — before they become visible in the performance counters. The PI controller with hysteresis deadband is the torque regulator that keeps the engine in the laminar-flow regime.

The math is deliberately simple because the hardware is deliberately complex:

$$
\begin{align*}
x_{k|k-1} &= Fx_{k-1|k-1} + Bu_{k-1} \\
P_{k|k-1} &= FP_{k-1|k-1}F^T + Q \\
K_k &= P_{k|k-1}H^T (HP_{k|k-1}H^T + R)^{-1} \\
x_{k|k} &= x_{k|k-1} + K_k(z_k - Hx_{k|k-1}) \\
P_{k|k} &= (I - K_kH)P_{k|k-1}
\end{align*}
$$

Where the state vector contains position, velocity, and acceleration of the instruction “rotor.” The measurement \(z_k\) is the atomic counter delta. The control input \(u\) is the throttle fraction Q.

This is not GPU programming as it is taught in universities. This is **control theory** applied to the most parallel machine humanity has ever built. It is the difference between telling the engine “run as hard as you can” and telling it “run exactly as hard as the load requires, with 50 µs of predictive stall avoidance.”

We have already proven the sensor. The next 48 hours are about proving the actuator — the throttle that scales work linearly with Q while the kernel stays alive. Once that works, the Kalman observer, the PI controller, the hysteresis deadband, and the full self-calibrating adaptive governor drop in like Lego bricks.

The persistent-kernel problem on Blackwell is real, but it is an engineering detail, not a fundamental limit. Every modern control system has had to solve memory-coherence and visibility issues between the controller and the plant. We will solve this one the same way the motor-control community solved it in the 1980s: with explicit fences, host-controlled measurement windows, and a clean separation between the high-rate inner loop (the persistent kernel) and the medium-rate outer loop (the host governor).
